  "summary": "Crimson and gold Hawaiian feathered capes and helmets — military might and divine protection woven in birds.",
  "description": "ʻAhu ʻula are the feathered cloaks of Hawaiian high chiefs and warriors: nets of olonā fiber into which hundreds of thousands of tiny feathers were bundled — mostly red ʻiʻiwi and ʻapapane, with yellow feathers from the ʻōʻō and mamo so rare that a great cloak could take generations and whole bird populations. Kamehameha I's great cloak (Nāhiʻenaʻena's, now in the Bishop Museum) concentrated mana and literally deflected spear points. Closed crafting after the 1840s makes each surviving cloak a singular national treasure of Hawaiʻi.",
